From c845ff0bffdd6a68fc2839dc1c9c2e938db768b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emergent E3 <e3@nivxray.local>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:16:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Windows re-enrolment recovery: publish the repaired
 installer, its CI and the regression tests
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This baseline cannot build a Windows installer at all: agents/nivxforge-windows
carries only the PS1, the exclusions module and a 0.2.0-windows sensor. The
native packager, the journal and acquisition modules, the build script and the
Windows workflow are all absent, so the artifact DESKTOP-A9HGFJJ is running
(setup 1.0.0 + sensor 0.2.0-windows, built from the 2026-09-27..09-30 window)
cannot be reproduced or superseded from this repository.

What that installed artifact does on `install --re-enrol`: it issues an
asynchronous `sc.exe stop`, copies over the image immediately, and has no
rollback — so a failure after step 1 leaves NivXForgeSensor STOPPED and never
reaches `sc create`/`sc start`. A failed re-enrolment blinds a healthy
endpoint. Its token also arrives on argv.

Published here, unchanged from the engineering tree that tested them:

  nivxforge_setup.py              stop-and-prove-STOPPED, image-release rename
                                  probe, bounded waits that fail closed,
                                  _restore_service rollback wrapped around
                                  steps 1-4 in `except BaseException`, and
                                  `--token-stdin` as the only secret interface
                                  (`--token` is absent from the parser).
  nivxforge_sensor.py             0.2.0-windows -> 0.3.0-windows
  nivxforge_journal.py            frozen-journal selftest the CI gate requires
  nivxforge_content_acquisition.py, nivxforge_delivery_counters.py
  build/build_windows_installer.ps1   stamps commit/run id/SHA256 provenance
  .github/workflows/windows-sensor-installer.yml   builds ON WINDOWS and
                                  proves the artifact about itself: build
                                  provenance, self SHA256, refusal of unknown
                                  flags, real SCM lifecycle, and Gate 4
                                  "re-enrol against a RUNNING service" with
                                  real NTFS locks.
  test_p0_windows_reenrol_staging_race.py   18 tests, including the live
                                  DESKTOP failure reproduced and rolled back.
  test_p0_win_reenrol_recovery.py  7 behavioural tests: REVOKED endpoint +
                                  fresh single-use token -> same endpoint_id,
                                  ACTIVE, delivery counters and timestamps
                                  preserved; and the processor-id fallback
                                  risk pinned as a test.

No artifact is claimed. Building one is this workflow's job, on Windows, from
a published commit. No deployment, no credential, no change to the laptop.

SANITIZED FOR PUBLICATION: the two Windows test files carry synthetic fixture
identifiers here (tenant, endpoint_id, hostname, processor id). The engineering
copies name the real laptop; this repository is public, and a customer tenant
and endpoint id do not belong in it. Only those literals differ — the asserted
behaviour is identical, and the published copies pass unchanged.
